.TH SHARDPOST-SYSTEM 1 "2026-10-02" "ShardPost 0.48.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpost-system \- place the store-wide state in a system pool, check it, take it back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system status
.RB [ \-\-deep ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system init \-\-pool
.I pool
.RB [ \-\-confirm
.IR run ]
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system migrate \-\-to
.I pool
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RB [ \-\-parallel
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-force\-registrations ]
.RB [ \-\-confirm
.IR run ]
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system verify
.RB [ \-\-source\-only
.RB [ \-\-delete\-surplus
.B \-\-confirm
.IR run ]]
.RB [ \-\-parallel
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system abort \-\-run
.I run
.RB [ \-\-delete\-copies ]
.RB [ \-\-delete\-surplus ]
.RB [ \-\-confirm
.IR run ]
.RB [ \-\-parallel
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system revert \-\-run
.I run
.B \-\-confirm
.I run
.RB [ \-\-delete\-surplus ]
.RB [ \-\-parallel
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B system cleanup \-\-run
.I run
.B \-\-confirm
.I run
.RB [ \-\-min\-age
.IR duration ]
.RB [ \-\-delete\-surplus ]
.RB [ \-\-parallel
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The store's own namespaces
.RB ( ~admin :
admin store, jobs, registrations, auth flush log, queues;
.BR ~accounts :
account directory and its queues;
.BR ~learning :
the indexes of the learning queue;
.BR ~server :
server metadata) live in the index pool, or, with
.IR ceph.system_pool ,
in a replicated pool of their own. These commands of the server binary
put them there and take them back. They talk to the pools directly
with the server's configuration and Ceph credentials, need no running
server and no admin socket, and run in a Kubernetes Job of their own
.RB ( hack/system\-job.yaml ).
They never register in the admin store. Each prints a report as JSON on
standard output; the log goes to standard error.
.PP
Which pool holds the state is decided by two objects, not by the
configuration: the fence
.B ~admin/system.moved
in the index pool and the mark
.B ~admin/system.pool
in the system pool, both naming the pools by id and the cluster by
fsid. A server whose
.I ceph.system_pool
does not match them refuses to start, and one that runs ends within a
minute once they change (see
.BR docs/architecture/storage.md ,
"System pool and placement").
.PP
The commands that write hold the
.I tool lease
in the fence for as long as they work: a second tool is refused at once,
naming the holder. The lease is renewed every 30 seconds; a tool that
could not renew it for 90 seconds stops every write at once and ends
with exit code 1 (running it again continues). A lease whose holder
died is taken over with
.B \-\-wait
once its holder was seen idle for three minutes on the taker's own
clock; use that only when the Job or pod of the old holder is known to
be gone, since a tool that was stopped (SIGSTOP, a frozen container) can
still send operations it prepared once it wakes up. Never pause the Job
of a tool. A lease whose last renewal lies more than 30 seconds ahead of
the taker's clock is refused: run every host with NTP. The tools run
with an OSD operation timeout of at most 60 seconds and refuse a
.I ceph.client_options
that sets more.
.PP
Every write command is resumable: run it again after an error, a crash
or a deleted Job, and it continues where it stopped. Destructive steps
need the run they belong to named with
.BR \-\-confirm .
.SS status
Shows the fence, the mark, the run state of a migration and the
registrations of the processes in the admin store (in the system pool
once the fence says so). With
.B \-\-deep
it also counts the objects of the store's namespaces in both pools and
of every quarantine (it lists the pools). It only reads.
.SS init
Initializes the system pool of a new cluster:
.I pool
must be replicated and empty, and the index pool must hold nothing of
the store's own namespaces (else the cluster exists: migrate it). The
init creates the fence, writes the completion markers of the backfills
into the empty indexes (so that the admin store and the learning queue
read without a listing from the first start), writes the mark (the
commit) and sets the fence to
.BR native .
An init that ran before ends at once without writing. With
.B \-\-confirm
.I run
it deletes the content of an earlier run in the system pool (a retired
mark and its copies, the run state of an aborted migration, the heads of
an aborted init) and that run's quarantine. Then set
.I ceph.system_pool
on the server, the push worker and the scrub CronJob.
.SS migrate
Moves the store's own namespaces of an existing cluster into the system
pool
.IR pool ,
in a maintenance window with every server, push worker, scrub, backfill
and sweep stopped. The phases, recorded in the run state
.B ~admin/system.migrate
of the system pool:
.TP
.B M0
the preconditions, after the lease was taken: the target is a pool of
its own, keeps omap (a probe) and holds nothing but this run's work (or,
confirmed, an earlier run's copies, which are deleted with that run's
quarantine after the fence is set); no registration of a process that
runs (seen within three minutes and not stopped), no valid lease of the
push worker or of a running job, no held lock, no watcher on the objects
every server watches
.RB ( auth\-flush ,
.BR ix.jobs/claimable/00 ,
.BR ix.learning/00 ,
.BR ix.submission/00 ;
each notified with a 2 second timeout).
.TP
.B M1
the fence becomes
.B migrating
(servers refuse from here on), the run state is created.
.TP
.B M2
one listing of the index pool: the source.
.TP
.B M3
the copy, in chunks of 4 MiB, with the xattrs (not the lock state) and
the omap, and a SHA-256 digest per object (not repeated once M4 is
done; a copy that stopped half way is replaced).
.TP
.B M4
the target holds exactly the source, both match the digests.
.TP
.B M5
the old objects move into the quarantine
.IB ns .moved/ run
of the index pool, each copy checked before its original goes; every
old index head is saved in the run state and poisoned (format 1000), so
that a server before 0.47.0 fails on it instead of reading an empty
place.
.TP
.B M6
the mark: the commit.
.TP
.B M7
the fence becomes
.BR done ;
the report goes into the run state.
.PP
A migration that ended is a no-op that prints its report again.
.B \-\-check
runs M0 without the lease and writes nothing but the probe: registrations,
leases, watchers and held locks (the worker locks of the running servers)
are reported with
.B blocking
false, not refused (the check before the processes are stopped); a
target that is not empty, a failed probe or another wrong precondition
still refuses. The migration itself refuses all of them.
.SS verify
Without
.BR \-\-source\-only :
compares the source of the migration (the old place, the quarantine, the
saved heads) with the copy in the system pool, object by object. Run it
before the servers use the system pool; once they write there, use
.BR \-\-source\-only .
With
.BR \-\-source\-only :
checks the old place after a migration: the store's namespaces of the
index pool may hold the fence, the poisoned heads (unchanged, by the
digest of M5) and anything under
.B ~accounts/sweep/
(the sweep stays in the index pool), nothing else; the quarantine must
have the digest of M4 until
.B cleanup
deleted it. Anything else is a surplus object, written by a process that
does not know the system pool; find out which one, then delete them with
.B \-\-delete\-surplus \-\-confirm
.I run
(under the lease).
.SS abort
Takes a migration (or an init) back before the servers use the system
pool. The first call records the phase it found; a later call
continues by it. Before M4: only the fence becomes
.BR reverted .
From M4 on: the system pool must still have the digest of M4 (else a
server used it: use
.BR revert );
the quarantine is copied back to the original names, the heads get
their saved values, surplus objects at the old place refuse (or are
deleted with
.B \-\-delete\-surplus
and
.BR \-\-confirm ),
the old place is checked against the digest of M4, the mark is retired
and the fence becomes
.BR reverted .
The quarantine is kept: delete it with
.B cleanup
after the minimum age, or with the next run's
.BR \-\-confirm .
An abort of a run that is aborted already checks the old place against
the digest of M4 again and repairs it from the quarantine (a late write
of a tool that was stopped);
.B cleanup
refuses to delete the quarantine while the old place differs.
.B \-\-delete\-copies
with
.B \-\-confirm
also deletes the copies in the system pool, after the fence is reverted
(run it again with the same options if it stopped there); the run state
and the mark stay as the record of the run. Else the next run deletes
the copies when its
.B \-\-confirm
names this run.
.SS revert
Takes a committed migration or init back, in a maintenance window: the
system pool is the truth. With every process stopped (the checks of M0,
on the system pool), the fence becomes
.B migrating
in the direction
.BR revert ,
every object of the system pool (but the placement objects) is copied
to its name in the index pool, overwriting what differs, the poisoned
heads included; objects of the index pool that the system pool does not
have refuse until
.B \-\-delete\-surplus
deletes them; the index pool is checked against a fresh digest of the
system pool, the quarantine is deleted, the mark retired and the fence
set to
.BR reverted .
Then clear
.I ceph.system_pool
everywhere. The copies stay in the system pool, retired, until a new
init or migration deletes them.
.SS cleanup
Deletes the quarantine of a migration once its fence is
.BR done ,
its mark active, the run at least
.B \-\-min\-age
old and the old place checks out as with
.BR "verify \-\-source\-only" .
The fence and the poisoned heads stay for good; the fence records
.BR cleaned_at ,
and from then on
.B verify \-\-source\-only
expects no quarantine. It also takes an aborted run (fence
.BR reverted )
and deletes only its quarantine.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
The server configuration (default: the environment variable
.BR SHARDPOST_CONFIG ).
.IR ceph.index_pool ,
.IR ceph.data_pool ,
.I ceph.user
and the Rook secret apply as for a server.
.I ceph.system_pool
should be empty (the tools take the system pool from
.BR \-\-pool ,
.B \-\-to
or the fence); a value that names another pool than the command is
refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-pool " pool"
.RB ( init )
The system pool to initialize.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " pool"
.RB ( migrate )
The system pool to migrate into.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " run"
.RB ( abort ,
.BR revert ,
.BR cleanup )
The run to work on, as
.B status
shows it (the fence's
.BR run ).
.TP
.BI \-\-confirm " run"
Confirms a destructive step by naming the run it belongs to: deleting an
earlier run's copies and quarantine
.RB ( init ,
.BR migrate ),
surplus objects or copies
.RB ( verify ,
.BR abort ),
a revert or a cleanup (required for both).
.TP
.B \-\-check
.RB ( migrate )
Only the preconditions, reporting running processes (registrations,
leases, watchers, held locks) instead of refusing them.
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel " n"
Objects copied or hashed at a time (16; 1 to 64, other values are
refused; not for
.B status
and
.BR init ). The tool raises the
objecter throttles of its connection to let them start
.RI ( objecter_inflight_ops
at least 4 per worker,
.I objecter_inflight_op_bytes
at least 4 MiB per worker) unless
.I ceph.client_options
sets them; values set there that are too small are refused.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-registrations
.RB ( migrate )
Ignore registrations of processes that seem to run. Only for processes
known to be dead (a pod killed without its orderly end); the report and
the log
.RB ( event=system_force_registration )
name every registration ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-source\-only
.RB ( verify )
Check the old place after the migration.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-surplus
.RB ( verify
with
.BR \-\-source\-only ,
.BR abort ,
.BR revert ,
.BR cleanup )
Delete the surplus objects at the old place (with
.BR \-\-confirm ).
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-copies
.RB ( abort )
Delete the copies in the system pool (with
.BR \-\-confirm ).
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-age " duration"
.RB ( cleanup )
How long ago the run must have ended (168h).
.TP
.B \-\-deep
.RB ( status )
Count the objects of both pools.
.TP
.B \-\-wait
Take over the lease of another tool once it was seen idle for three
minutes. Only when that tool's Job or pod is known to be gone.
.SH ROLES
None: the commands use the Ceph credentials of the server
.RB ( client.shardpost
in the manifests), not the admin API. Its caps must include the system
pool.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done (also: nothing to do).
.TP
.B 1
An error, a lost lease or an interruption; run the command again to
continue.
.TP
.B 2
Usage, or a precondition that does not hold: a running process, a lease
held by another tool, a target that is not empty, surplus objects
without
.BR \-\-delete\-surplus ,
a missing
.BR \-\-confirm .
The report names it; nothing was changed that the next run cannot take
up. Right after the processes were stopped this is normal: the push
worker's lease is valid for up to 90 seconds, job leases for up to 60,
registrations without an orderly end for up to three minutes. Run it
again after 90 seconds.
.TP
.B 3
A check found the pools different from what they must be: a digest that
does not match, an object that must not be there. Nothing is
overwritten that must not be.
.SH EXAMPLES
The check in normal operation, before the maintenance window:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system migrate \-\-to shardpost\-system \-\-check
.fi
.RE
.PP
The migration, with every process stopped; the run id is in the report
.RB ( run ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system migrate \-\-to shardpost\-system
shardpost system verify
.fi
.RE
.PP
After the servers run with
.IR ceph.system_pool :
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system verify \-\-source\-only
shardpost system status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Seven days later, the quarantine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system cleanup \-\-run 4f2a9c1e07b3 \-\-confirm 4f2a9c1e07b3
.fi
.RE
.PP
A migration that failed its checks, taken back before the servers
started:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system abort \-\-run 4f2a9c1e07b3
.fi
.RE
.PP
Back to the index pool after the switch, with the processes stopped:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system revert \-\-run 4f2a9c1e07b3 \-\-confirm 4f2a9c1e07b3
.fi
.RE
.PP
A new cluster:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shardpost system init \-\-pool shardpost\-system
.fi
.RE
